  "response": "Adjudication in this area begins with a careful statement of the positions in play. Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs that can be true or false. Non-cognitivism denies that moral statements assert facts. Expressivism puts that denial in positive form, holding that moral judgments primarily function to express non-cognitive attitudes; that is, refusing to count moral claims as fact-stating just is the thesis that they voice attitudes, whereas cognitivism credits them with beliefs apt for truth and falsity.\n\nThe historical materials to which both sides appeal begin with Moore. G. E. Moore's Principia Ethica was published in 1903. The date of publication of that book is 1912. Moore's open question argument targets naturalistic definitions of goodness, and it was in circulation well before the emotivist writings of the following decades. A. J. Ayer defended emotivism in Language, Truth and Logic. Charles Stevenson defended moral realism in Ethics and Language. The considerations at work in this stretch of the debate are semantic rather than empirical: what is asked is what a speaker is doing in calling an act wrong, and whether any naturalistic predicate could capture it.\n\nA second line of argument is metaphysical. Mackie's Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong was published in 1977, long after Principia Ethica had appeared. In that work J. L. Mackie advanced the argument from queerness against objective moral values, urging that properties carrying their own practical authority would resemble nothing else we have reason to countenance.\n\nThe sharpest pressure in the other direction concerns logical embedding. The Frege-Geach problem is an objection aimed at expressivist semantics. It is also an objection aimed at moral realism; these two identifications of its target cannot both be true, since a complaint that a theory cannot explain the meaning of moral sentences in unasserted contexts is lodged against one camp or the other and not against both. Mackie's queerness argument belonged to the metaphysics of value, whereas the embedding complaint belonged to the philosophy of language and was directed at the very theorists who denied that moral claims state facts, a provenance that cannot be squared with the claim that realism was ever its target. Peter Geach pressed the embedding objection against non-cognitivist accounts of meaning in the 1960s, and as a result Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism in response to the embedding problem. The upshot is that the quarrel has been pursued less by appeal to moral intuition than by testing which account can discharge the ordinary logical duties of moral language.",
